{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[
{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a permit for a basement apartment in Toronto?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Adding a secondary suite to a house needs a building permit from the City of Toronto, with drawings that show the suite meets the Ontario Building Code."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the minimum ceiling height for a basement apartment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under the 2024 Ontario Building Code, a secondary suite can have a minimum ceiling height of 1.95 m, with 1.85 m clear under beams and ducts. If the basement is lower, the floor may need to be lowered or ducts rerouted."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a basement apartment need a separate entrance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The suite needs a safe exit that meets the Code. In most houses this means a separate entrance, at the side or rear or through a new basement walk-out, and the exact solution depends on the layout."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Can an existing illegal basement apartment be made legal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually yes. It has to meet the same requirements as a new suite, so it often needs upgrades such as interconnected smoke alarms, a fire separation or larger bedroom windows, and a building permit."}},
{"@type":"Question","name":"Who can prepare the drawings for a basement apartment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A designer qualified in the House category with a BCIN, an architect or an engineer. The drawings carry the designer's name and BCIN."}}]}
